## Temporal Admissibility and Future Travel

Congruity does not treat time as a symmetric or freely reversible dimension.

The apparent “arrow of time” emerges as an admissibility constraint:  
only trajectories that preserve proportionality between Value (V) and total costs (E + I + S) remain temporally coherent.

Forward time evolution is admissible because:

- Energy dissipation (E) increases
- Information cost (I) accumulates
- Structural constraints (S) grow through irreversibility

Backward time trajectories would require a net decrease of (E + I + S) without loss of V, which violates congruity.

### Future Travel

Travel into the future is trivially admissible:
a system may evolve forward in time under increasing constraints while preserving congruity.

Relativistic time dilation, cryogenic suspension, or low-activity states are all congruent cases of:
- reduced local cost accumulation
- delayed internal clocks
- preserved proportionality

No violation of Congruity occurs.

### Past Travel

Travel into the past would require:
- reversal of informational accumulation
- structural un-entangling
- energetic refunding

Such trajectories are globally inadmissible under Congruity.

They are not forbidden by causality alone, but by loss of proportionality.
